  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Increased capacity of ILO&apos;s constituents to improve workplace compliance</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Ethiopia&#x2019;s rapidly expanding garment sector has created employment opportunities but also revealed persistent challenges in labour relations, including weak compliance with international labour standards, limited worker voice, and ineffective workplace dialogue mechanisms. In many factories, worker&#x2013;management interactions remain informal and inconsistent, leading to unresolved grievances, workplace disputes, and gaps in compliance. 
To address these challenges, CPO ETH176 strengthens the capacity of government, workers&#x2019; and employers&#x2019; organizations to improve workplace compliance and social dialogue through the ONE ILO SIRAYE programme and related initiatives. The programme supports the establishment and strengthening of structured bipartite worker&#x2013;management committees in participating factories, provides training on workplace cooperation, industrial relations, and dispute prevention, and offers advisory services to enhance dialogue mechanisms and grievance handling systems. It also documents and shares good practices to inform sector-wide approaches. 
As a result, workplace cooperation is expected to shift from ad hoc interactions to structured, regular, and effective dialogue mechanisms. Improved worker&#x2013;management communication and functioning committees will enable timely resolution of workplace issues, reduce disputes, and strengthen labour relations. Over time, this will contribute to improved compliance with labour standards, wider adoption of collective bargaining and workplace cooperation practices, and better working conditions, while enhancing productivity and resilience in the garment sector. The primary beneficiaries include the Ministry of Labour and Skills, workers&#x2019; and employers&#x2019; organizations, factory management, and workers in participating garment factories. Priority groups include youth, refugees, and vulnerable workers in supply chains. Indirect beneficiaries include the broader garment sector, which will benefit from stronger labour relations,</narrative>
  </description>
